/* Pass 21B: canon-exact Danse and Severus revisions. */
(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-12":{"number":12,"title":"Vertibird Weather","content":"Vertibirds had weather.\n\nNo one warned me about that.\n\nOn the ground, weather was rain, wind, heat, radstorms, and the occasional piece of metal traveling fast enough to count as a local climate. In a vertibird, weather was all of those things plus gravity developing opinions.\n\nDanse loved flying.\n\nHe would deny this if asked directly, so I did not ask directly.\n\nI watched instead.\n\nThe moment we cleared the Prydwen’s flight deck, something in his shoulders relaxed. He sat near the open side with one hand on the overhead grip, scanning the city below. The wind pulled at his hair. His expression was alert but peaceful.\n\n“You like this,” I said.\n\nHe looked over.\n\n“Operational mobility is useful.”\n\n“That was not a denial.”\n\n“I don’t know what you want from me.”\n\n“Joy.”\n\n“That seems excessive.”\n\nThe vertibird hit turbulence.\n\nMy stomach attempted desertion.\n\nDanse caught the front of my harness before I could slide half a foot across the bench.\n\n“Secure yourself.”\n\n“I am secured.”\n\n“Poorly.”\n\n“Blame whoever designed a seatbelt for people wearing combat armor.”\n\nHe leaned closer and tightened the strap himself.\n\nHis knuckles brushed my ribs through my coat.\n\nI forgot the turbulence.\n\nDanse noticed my sudden stillness.\n\nHis hands paused.\n\nWe were close enough that I could see a tiny nick in his lower lip, probably from shaving too quickly.\n\n“Too tight?” he asked.\n\n“No.”\n\nHis eyes remained on mine a fraction too long.\n\nThen he sat back.\n\n“Good.”\n\nThe mission was simple by Brotherhood standards: survey a collapsed research building, recover data, kill anything hostile enough to object.\n\nBy Commonwealth standards, that meant three hours underground, two packs of ferals, one automated turret, and a ceiling collapse that trapped us in a laboratory with a door fused shut.\n\nDanse examined it.\n\n“Thermal damage.”\n\n“Can you open it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe tried.\n\nThe door did not move.\n\nI leaned against the wall.\n\n“Outstanding.”\n\nHe gave me a look.\n\n“We need leverage.”\n\nWe found a length of pipe. Together we wedged it into the seam.\n\n“On three,” he said.\n\n“You mean one, two, three, push? Or one, two, push on three?”\n\nDanse closed his eyes.\n\n“Why would anyone push before three?”\n\n“You would be amazed.”\n\n“On three.”\n\nWe pushed.\n\nThe pipe bent.\n\nThe door remained.\n\nDanse swore.\n\nI laughed.\n\n“This amuses you?”\n\n“A little.”\n\n“We’re trapped.”\n\n“With supplies, weapons, and a radio.”\n\n“The signal won’t penetrate this depth.”\n\n“Still funny that you swore.”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\n“You’ve heard me swear.”\n\n“Not at a door.”\n\n“That’s your standard?”\n\n“I contain multitudes.”\n\nHe sat on an overturned cabinet.\n\nFor twenty minutes we tried every practical solution.\n\nThen we stopped.\n\nThe room became quiet.\n\nAn emergency light pulsed over us, red then dark, red then dark.\n\nDanse removed his gloves and flexed his hands.\n\n“Blisters?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Let me see.”\n\n“I’m fine.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\nHe held out one hand.\n\nThere was a shallow cut across his palm from the bent pipe.\n\nI pulled a small antiseptic packet from my bag.\n\n“It’s nothing.”\n\n“So was my coat at ArcJet, according to you.”\n\n“That was different.”\n\n“How?”\n\nHe had no answer.\n\nI cleaned the cut.\n\nHis hand was rough from weapons, tools, and years of work. He watched my fingers move over his palm with an intensity that made the room feel warmer.\n\n“You know,” I said, because silence was becoming dangerous, “for someone covered in advanced military technology most of the time, you get injured in extremely mundane ways.”\n\n“Occupational hazard.”\n\n“Pipe doors.”\n\n“Very dangerous.”\n\n“Coffee.”\n\n“I have never been injured by coffee.”\n\n“Your taste has.”\n\nHe laughed.\n\nActually laughed.\n\nA low, surprised sound that seemed to escape before he could stop it.\n\nI looked up.\n\nDanse looked almost offended.\n\n“Don’t.”\n\n“I didn’t say anything.”\n\n“You’re looking at me.”\n\n“I do that sometimes.”\n\nHis hand remained in mine.\n\nNeither of us mentioned it.\n\nEventually we found another route: a maintenance crawlspace above the laboratory.\n\nDanse hated it.\n\nPower armor had to stay behind while we squeezed through the narrow shaft. Without it, he moved more quietly but seemed oddly exposed.\n\nHalfway through, the metal beneath me groaned.\n\n“Stop,” he said.\n\nI froze.\n\nHe was behind me.\n\n“Move your weight to the crossbeam.”\n\n“Which one?”\n\nHis hand settled at my hip to guide me.\n\nEvery thought I had vanished.\n\n“There,” he said.\n\nHis voice was close to my ear.\n\nI shifted carefully.\n\nThe panel held.\n\nDanse removed his hand at once.\n\n“Continue.”\n\nNeither of us spoke until we reached the other side.\n\nWe returned to the Prydwen after dark with the data secured and the damaged power armor retrieved.\n\nAs the vertibird climbed, Danse sat beside me instead of across from me.\n\nOur shoulders touched whenever the aircraft banked.\n\nThis time neither of us moved away.\n\nBelow us, the Commonwealth looked almost beautiful.\n\nDanse watched the horizon.\n\nI watched him.\n\nWhen he caught me, I expected a question.\n\nInstead he looked back at the city and said, “The view is better from this side.”\n\nI knew he was giving me an excuse.\n\nI took it.\n\n“Yes,” I said. “It is.”\n\nBefore we boarded, the ground crew stopped us beside a stack of replacement parts. A young technician had discovered that a crate marked *hydraulic fittings* contained three ordinary bolts and a damaged hose coupling. The manifest had been signed by somebody who had never opened the lid.\n\n“We'll sort it when we get back,” the pilot said, already watching the storm clouds.\n\nDanse checked the tag. “If that coupling is the part you intend to replace in flight, we will not be departing with it.”\n\nThe pilot's expression tightened. “Paladin, I know my aircraft.”\n\n“Then you know what happens when a pressure line is repaired with a component whose rating you cannot establish.”\n\nFor a moment the air between them was as sharp as the fuel smell. I expected him to pull rank. Instead Danse handed the manifest back to the technician and asked which fittings had been verified and which remained untested.\n\nThe pilot checked the maintenance record, swore quietly, and agreed to wait for a correctly labeled spare. Nobody was delighted. A supply run delayed by ordinary caution had none of the glory of rescuing a vertibird from a crash.\n\nWhile the new part was carried out, I stood beside Danse in the wind.\n\n“You looked like you were about to order the whole flight line to attention.”\n\n“I considered it.”\n\n“And?”\n\n“The pilot is responsible for the machine. The technician is responsible for the part. I am responsible for not pretending either of those facts stops being true because I dislike being delayed.”\n\nHe looked at me, serious and a little embarrassed.\n\n“You could have said that without the uniform.”\n\n“I'm working on it.”\n\n\nOn the return flight from our first supply run, the weather turned mean.\n\nThe pilot warned us about turbulence. Danse answered with the calm acknowledgement of a man who considered gravity a subordinate officer.\n\nI tightened my harness.\n\n“You can admit you hate this too.”\n\n“I don’t hate vertibirds.”\n\nThe aircraft dropped hard enough to lift my stomach into my throat.\n\nDanse’s hand clamped around the overhead strap.\n\n“Your knuckles disagree.”\n\n“My knuckles are maintaining stability.”\n\n“Heroically.”\n\nThe pilot laughed over the intercom.\n\nDanse glared toward the cockpit.\n\nAnother gust shoved us sideways.\n\nA crate broke loose from its floor clamp.\n\nDanse was out of his seat before I registered it. He caught the crate with one boot, shoved it back, and locked the damaged clamp with a spare length of webbing.\n\n“Stay seated,” he ordered.\n\n“You are also not seated.”\n\n“I’m securing equipment.”\n\n“I was going to help.”\n\n“And now you are going to remain secured.”\n\nI crossed my arms.\n\nHe returned to his seat, buckled in, and looked at me.\n\n“What?”\n\n“That was very romantic.”\n\nHis expression went blank.\n\n“The crate?”\n\n“No. Ordering me into a chair.”\n\n“I was preventing injury.”\n\n“Exactly. Swoon.”\n\nHe stared for three seconds.\n\nThen his mouth betrayed him.\n\nThe smile was small, but real.\n\n“You have a strange definition of romance.”\n\n“You gave me a laser rifle and called it recognition of service.”\n\n“That was not romantic.”\n\n“Your track record is against you.”\n\nThe aircraft dipped again.\n\nThis time my stomach lost the argument entirely.\n\nI went quiet.\n\nDanse noticed at once.\n\n“You all right?”\n\n“Fine.”\n\nHe gave me the look I usually gave him.\n\n“That is not a useful answer.”\n\n“I’m reconsidering my relationship with the sky.”\n\nHis expression softened.\n\n“Look at me.”\n\nI did.\n\n“Not outside. The horizon movement makes it worse.”\n\n“You have experience with this?”\n\n“First deployment. I was sick through half the flight.”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“Paladin Danse gets airsick?”\n\n“Got.”\n\n“Oh, this is precious information.”\n\n“If you repeat it, I will deny everything.”\n\n“Noted.”\n\nAnother rough pocket shook the cabin.\n\nDanse kept talking.\n\nNot about the flight. About maintenance schedules. About how Brotherhood pilots learned to read crosswinds around ruined skyscrapers. About a mechanic at the Citadel who once painted a deathclaw on a vertibird’s nose and got three days of disciplinary duty for it.\n\nThe stories were not particularly important.\n\nThat was the point.\n\nDanse kept talking because he had noticed silence made the vertibird worse for me.\n\nHe told me about a lancer who once tried to land during a dust storm and came down on a pre-War billboard. He described the resulting lecture from Kells with such precise mimicry that I stared at him.\n\n“You do impressions?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“That was absolutely an impression.”\n\n“It was an accurate reconstruction.”\n\n“Of his voice.”\n\n“Relevant to the story.”\n\nThe aircraft dropped hard enough to make my stomach climb into my throat. My hand tightened over his.\n\nDanse did not comment.\n\nInstead he continued. “The billboard said ‘See Scenic Boston.’”\n\nI laughed despite the turbulence.\n\n“That’s cruel.”\n\n“The lancer was unharmed.”\n\n“So you’re allowed to laugh?”\n\n“I did not say I laughed.”\n\n“You remember the sign.”\n\n“That proves nothing.”\n\nAnother shudder. His thumb pressed once against the side of my hand.\n\n“Look at me,” he said.\n\nI did.\n\n“Not outside. The horizon makes it worse when we bank.”\n\n“How do you know?”\n\n“I’ve transported enough recruits.”\n\n“And how many held your hand?”\n\nHis ears reddened.\n\n“None.”\n\n“Excellent. I enjoy exclusive training benefits.”\n\nDanse looked toward the open door with tremendous interest.\n\nBut he did not pull away.\n\nHis voice gave me something steady to hold onto.\n\nBy the time the Prydwen came into view, my stomach had mostly returned to its assigned location.\n\n“Better?” he asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI looked at our hands.\n\nAt some point during the worst of the turbulence, his had ended up palm-down on the bench between us.\n\nMine rested over it.\n\nNeither of us seemed able to identify when that happened.\n\nDanse looked too.\n\nHis thumb moved once beneath my fingers.\n\n“Atmospheric conditions,” I said.\n\n“Obviously.”\n\n“Safety procedure.”\n\n“Clearly.”\n\nNeither of us moved our hands until the landing gear touched the deck.\n","word_count":1825},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-25":{"number":25,"title":"Missing","content":"Maxson gave the order without raising his voice.\n\nThat made it worse.\n\nPaladin Danse was a synth. A security threat. An abomination created by the Institute. His service record did not matter. His loyalty did not matter. His years in the Brotherhood did not matter.\n\nFind him.\n\nExecute him.\n\nReturn with his holotags.\n\nI stood in the command deck listening to the machinery of Danse’s worldview turn against him.\n\n“Was there evidence he knew?” I asked.\n\nMaxson’s eyes hardened.\n\n“That is irrelevant.”\n\n“It is extremely relevant.”\n\n“He is a synth.”\n\n“He is Danse.”\n\n“That name belongs to a lie.”\n\nAnger came up so fast I tasted metal.\n\n“He bled for you.”\n\n“So have thousands.”\n\n“He led your people. He recovered your technology. He lost half his squad carrying out Brotherhood orders.”\n\n“And if the Institute planted him to do exactly that?”\n\nThat stopped me.\n\nNot because I believed it.\n\nBecause fear was persuasive when it wore uncertainty.\n\nMaxson saw the hesitation.\n\n“That is why the order stands.”\n\nI left before I said something that would get me shot.\n\nHaylen was waiting near the flight deck.\n\nShe had packed supplies into my bag.\n\n“RadAway. Stimpaks. Fusion cells. Food.”\n\nI took it.\n\n“You’re helping me disobey a direct order.”\n\n“I’m helping you complete a mission.”\n\n“Haylen.”\n\nHer mouth trembled.\n\n“Bring him back if you can.”\n\nI did not promise.\n\nPromises felt dangerous.\n\nHaylen stopped me again at the last hatch.\n\n“Don't tell him I packed the bag,” she said.\n\n“Because Maxson would punish you?”\n\n“Because Danse will turn it into another thing he thinks he has to repay.”\n\nI tightened the strap. She looked exhausted. The scribe who usually corrected Rhys's inventory numbers without looking up was struggling to keep her voice level.\n\n“You could come with me,” I said.\n\n“No.” She answered before I'd finished. “I can't be seen leaving with you, and I won't pretend I can solve this by sneaking in beside you. If they ask, I know that I gave you supplies. I can live with that choice.”\n\nI wanted to argue. She wouldn't be safer because I wished she were. She wouldn't be more loyal because I took her into the danger.\n\n“What if I find him and he doesn't want to come?” I asked.\n\n“Then you listen,” Haylen said. “He's been ordered around by people who say they're protecting him for long enough.”\n\nShe handed me a small folded sheet. It listed the medical supplies she knew Danse could use without guessing at doses or injuries she hadn't seen. Nothing on it said I would succeed.\n\nAt the flight deck, a mechanic was checking the vertibird fuel line. I waited until he waved me clear, then went on foot through the rain. Haylen watched from the hatch until the corner hid her.\n\nI carried her help without turning it into a promise to bring him home against his will.\n\n\nListening Post Bravo sat hidden beneath a ruined structure north of the city. I traveled alone because nobody else could know where I was going.\n\nThe Commonwealth seemed determined to slow me down.\n\nRain began by noon. Mirelurks forced me off the river road. A pack of ferals trapped me inside a laundromat for forty minutes.\n\nEvery delay fed the same thought.\n\nWhat if Danse obeyed before I got there?\n\nHe had told me what Brotherhood protocol meant.\n\nHe had told me about Cutler.\n\nHe had said dangerous things did not become safe because they were familiar.\n\nWould he apply that logic to himself?\n\nOf course he would.\n\nThat terrified me more than Maxson’s order.\n\nI reached the listening post after dark.\n\nThe entrance was concealed behind a security door half buried in concrete.\n\nDanse’s access code still worked.\n\nThe elevator descended too slowly.\n\nI could hear my own breathing.\n\nThe doors opened onto a narrow bunker corridor lit by red emergency lamps.\n\n“Danse?”\n\nNo answer.\n\nI moved deeper.\n\nA Brotherhood holotag lay on a table.\n\nHis.\n\nMy stomach turned.\n\nThen a voice came from the room beyond.\n\n“You shouldn’t have come.”\n\nI closed my eyes.\n\nAlive.\n\nI stepped through the doorway.\n\nDanse sat on a cot wearing his Brotherhood uniform without armor. His rifle leaned against the wall out of reach.\n\nHe looked terrible.\n\nNot injured.\n\nWorse.\n\nEmpty.\n\nI crossed the room before I remembered to be careful.\n\nHe stood.\n\n“Stop.”\n\nI stopped.\n\nHis eyes were red-rimmed from exhaustion.\n\n“How did you find me?”\n\n“Haylen.”\n\nHe looked away.\n\n“I told her not to interfere.”\n\n“She ignored an order. Terrible influence.”\n\nUsually that would have earned something.\n\nNothing.\n\nDanse looked at the holotag on the table.\n\n“Maxson sent you.”\n\nIt was not a question.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n“To execute me.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe word tore on the way out.\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n“Then do it.”\n\nEverything inside me stopped.\n\n“No.”\n\nHis expression hardened.\n\n“You have your orders.”\n\n“I said no.”\n\n“You swore an oath.”\n\n“I don’t care.”\n\n“I do.”\n\nHis voice cracked through the bunker.\n\nI stared at him.\n\nHe was shaking.\n\nNot visibly enough that anyone else might notice.\n\nI knew him now.\n\n“I’m a synth,” he said. “The exact thing we’ve been fighting.”\n\n“You’re Danse.”\n\n“That is not an answer.”\n\n“It is to me.”\n\nHis face twisted.\n\n“You don’t understand.”\n\n“I understand Maxson wants me to kill the man I—”\n\nI stopped.\n\nLove hovered there.\n\nNeither of us was ready for it.\n\nDanse heard it anyway.\n\nHis eyes closed.\n\n“Don’t,” he whispered.\n\nThe bunker suddenly felt like ArcJet again.\n\nDanse standing beneath a machine, ordering me to press the button.\n\nOnly this time there would be no power armor.\n\nAnd I had learned something since then.\n\nFollowing an order did not make the consequence easier to live with.\n\nI took one step closer.\n\n“No,” I said again. “Not this time.”\n\nThe road to Bravo took me past places Danse and I had crossed together.\n\nThat made the journey crueler.\n\nThe ruined service station where he had first admitted he was tired.\n\nThe bridge where we argued about Amos.\n\nA stretch of road where he had once corrected my spacing three times in five minutes.\n\nNow there was no armored shape six feet off my left side.\n\nI hated the empty space.\n\nNear dusk I found fresh laser burns on the shell of an old car.\n\nBrotherhood pattern.\n\nMy stomach dropped.\n\nI crouched beside the road and studied the marks.\n\nOne attacker. Maybe two. The shots had come from the west. Return fire had struck a concrete barrier at shoulder height.\n\nDanse had been here.\n\nI knew his shooting the way I knew his footsteps by then: controlled pairs, center mass, no wasted sweep.\n\nA dead raider lay behind the barrier with a laser burn through the chest.\n\nAlive, then.\n\nAlive recently.\n\nI stood too quickly and had to steady myself against the car.\n\n“Get a grip.”\n\nThe words sounded like him.\n\nThat helped more than I wanted to admit.\n\nI kept moving.\n\nAt the next ridge a Brotherhood vertibird crossed far to the south.\n\nI dropped beneath a concrete overhang and watched it pass.\n\nFor months, that sound had meant Danse’s people.\n\nNow it meant danger.\n\nI wondered what it felt like for him to hear it.\n\nThe thought turned my stomach again.\n\nBy full dark I had reached the ruined structure above Bravo.\n\nBefore entering, I sat on the broken steps and forced myself to eat half a ration bar.\n\nDanse would have told me I was useless if I arrived dehydrated and shaking.\n\nActually, he would have said *operationally compromised.*\n\nI drank water too.\n\nThen I took the holotag he had once handed me during power armor training—the spare tag he used to demonstrate how Brotherhood identification codes were stamped.\n\nI had kept it as a joke.\n\nThe metal felt heavy now.\n\nWhat makes a person?\n\nA number on a tag?\n\nA number in an Institute registry?\n\nA body grown instead of born?\n\nA memory that might have happened to someone else?\n\nNone of the answers I had pushed Danse toward seemed clean enough when the question wore his face.\n\nI was afraid.\n\nNot only that he might be dead.\n\nThat he might be dangerous.\n\nMaxson’s argument had lodged where I hated it most. What if there was a command hidden inside him? What if the Institute had built his loyalty as camouflage? What if love made me stupid?\n\nI let the fears exist.\n\nThen I asked myself one simpler question.\n\nWhat had Danse chosen when nobody was forcing him?\n\nHe had stopped for civilians.\n\nHe had buried his dead.\n\nHe had helped Amos after insulting him.\n\nHe had saved Nick before his doctrine could object.\n\nHe had filed a report to keep water running because he decided an order could wait.\n\nHe had held my hand in thunderstorms and pretended it was logistics.\n\nThose choices were evidence too.\n\nNot proof of safety.\n\nProof of personhood.\n\nI stood.\n\nThe closer I got to Bravo, the more Maxson’s words tried to become Danse’s voice in my head.\n\nMachine. Infiltrator. Threat.\n\nThen another memory answered.\n\nDanse at ArcJet, ordering me out of the engine chamber before the test fired.\n\nDanse carrying Haylen through dust after the floor collapse.\n\nDanse standing between Amos and a raider bullet before his prejudice had time to approve.\n\nNone of those memories proved he was incapable of danger.\n\nPeople were dangerous.\n\nThat was the point I had spent months trying to make to him.\n\nI stopped at the top of the hill and checked my weapon again.\n\nMy hands were shaking.\n\nLove did not make me certain.\n\nIt made uncertainty matter more.\n\nIf Danse had known he was an Institute agent, I would have to face that. If his memories were false, I would have to face that too. If he hated me for refusing the execution order, I would survive it.\n\nBut I would not kill him because a category made the decision easier.\n\nThe bunker door waited below.\n\nBefore entering the code, I drew my weapon.\n\nThat hurt.\n\nI did it anyway.\n\nLove was not pretending risk did not exist.\n\nLove was refusing to let fear become the only evidence admitted.\n\nThe lock accepted Haylen’s code.\n\nMetal scraped open.\n\nI stepped inside.\n\n“Danse?”\n\nMy voice echoed down the corridor.\n\nNothing answered.\n\nI tightened my grip on the weapon and headed for the elevator.\n\nWhatever waited below, I would look at the person in front of me before the category.\n\nI had asked Danse to learn that.\n\nNow it was my turn.\n","word_count":1753},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-47":{"number":47,"title":"A Practical Kind of Courage","content":"The trainee asked Danse why the workshop safety handbook had a section titled IDENTIFY UNTRUSTWORTHY PERSONNEL.\n\nThe question landed in the room like a dropped wrench.\n\nRenn was an adult apprentice from a border settlement who had come to Brasswatch for a short training placement. They were learning to maintain water pumps and repair simple metal fittings. They had proved patient with measurements, careful with tools, and completely uninterested in the idea that a former Paladin's reputation should end a conversation.\n\nDanse looked at the page.\n\n\"That section was copied from an older manual.\"\n\n\"Why is it still here?\" Renn asked.\n\nHe didn't answer immediately.\n\nI was at the next table checking the revised payment records. Ash was working in the adjoining room, where the private door now locked from the inside. They could hear the discussion, but nobody had asked them to participate.\n\nRenn tapped the page.\n\n\"It says unfamiliar people should be reported until their origin is confirmed. That sounds like a reason to watch everyone who comes through the Threadways.\"\n\nDanse rubbed a hand across his jaw.\n\n\"It was written for a military environment.\"\n\n\"Is the workshop a military environment?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then why are we teaching it?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe met my eyes, then looked back at the trainee.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nRenn blinked.\n\n\"I am?\"\n\n\"Yes. That section doesn't belong in this handbook.\"\n\nIt could have ended there, with a simple correction and everyone returning to work.\n\nBut Danse was staring at the page with the expression of someone who had discovered an old voice speaking through his own handwriting.\n\n\"I used to believe those procedures were necessary,\" he said. \"I enforced them.\"\n\nRenn waited.\n\n\"That doesn't make them correct,\" Danse continued. \"And it doesn't make the people affected by them responsible for making me feel better about changing.\"\n\nI set down the ledger.\n\nIt wasn't a polished apology. It was better.\n\nRenn looked at him.\n\n\"Then can we write a new rule?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThey pulled a blank sheet from the drawer.\n\nThe new section would concern actual safety behavior: handling hazardous materials, keeping exit routes clear, documenting threats without profiling visitors, asking consent before examining personal belongings, and protecting private information unless there was a specific, immediate danger.\n\nIt would not require anyone to disclose a homeworld, identity, or past allegiance merely to receive repair service.\n\nI helped check the wording.\n\n\"How do we make sure people actually follow it?\" Renn asked.\n\n\"Training,\" Danse said.\n\n\"And consequences,\" I added.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Consequences.\"\n\nBy noon, we had revised three pages.\n\nThe work revealed other old assumptions. One instruction required the person in charge to approve every emergency exit. That made sense for a locked armory and no sense for a public workshop where people might need to leave during a fire.\n\nAnother said workers should ignore a task interruption until given permission to stop. That was how injuries happened.\n\nRenn crossed it out.\n\nDanse watched them.\n\n\"Are you angry?\" he asked.\n\n\"At the old handbook? Yes.\"\n\n\"At me?\"\n\nRenn thought about it.\n\n\"A little. But mostly I want to know whether this place will be different.\"\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Then judge the changes we make.\"\n\nI could see how hard it was for him not to ask for forgiveness, not to explain his old reasoning until it sounded noble. He let the discomfort stay.\n\nBefore the test, Ash asked to read the revised handbook in the private workroom.\n\nRenn carried them a clean copy and waited outside while Ash looked through it. When they returned, two sections had been marked with dark pencil.\n\n\"You changed the question about where people come from,\" Ash said. \"But you still ask whether someone has a lawful reason to be here.\"\n\nDanse frowned.\n\n\"That line concerns workshop access.\"\n\n\"Then say workshop access. People can have an appointment or need an urgent repair without proving they belong in the valley.\"\n\nI watched Danse look at the sentence as though it had appeared on the page while he wasn't paying attention.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nHe crossed it out.\n\nRenn added a practical substitute: *Ask what service is needed and whether it can be provided safely. For hazardous equipment, explain specific training and access restrictions without demanding personal history.*\n\nAsh nodded.\n\n\"Better. And the complaint process?\"\n\nDanse showed them the line that directed complaints to the workshop supervisor.\n\n\"That's you,\" Ash said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"What happens if the complaint is about you?\"\n\nI saw the discomfort move across his face.\n\n\"Then it cannot stop with me.\"\n\nWe wrote a second path to the independent steward and included where to leave a sealed note. The procedure would be posted at the entrance, not hidden in the handbook after forty pages.\n\nDanse put the pencil down.\n\n\"I thought I was making the rules clearer.\"\n\n\"You are,\" Renn said. \"Because people can question them.\"\n\nAsh returned the copy without praising him.\n\nThey didn't owe him that.\n\nWhen the emergency exercise began, I noticed Danse left the marked copy on the front table where anyone could see the revisions. The old habit would have been to lock away a flawed procedure until it was perfect.\n\nToday the process itself was public, with a date beside the next review.\n\nIt was one more way this workshop differed from the place he'd once called home.\n\nThat afternoon we tested the new emergency procedure.\n\nPip played the role of someone arriving with a box of loose fittings and no appointment. Edda tested the public entrance. Renn simulated a small electrical fault using a disconnected training board. Ash chose to observe from the private workroom with the door closed.\n\nDanse did not order anyone to reveal where they came from.\n\nHe asked where help was needed.\n\nWhen Renn reported the simulated fault, the group used the accessible exit and left the tools behind. The test wasn't perfect. Someone had to move a cart out of the way. The alarm cord was too high for one visitor. We wrote both problems down.\n\n\"Again tomorrow,\" Danse said.\n\nRenn smiled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nAfter the shift, I found Danse alone in the storage shed looking at the old handbook. He had separated the discarded pages from those still useful. The paper smelled of oil and dust.\n\n\"Will you burn it?\" I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n\"I want to keep one copy as an example of what not to teach.\"\n\n\"With notes?\"\n\n\"With notes.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"Does it feel strange?\"\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nHe looked down at the title.\n\n\"There was a time when I thought courage meant obeying the right order despite fear. Later I thought it meant refusing an order that was wrong. Today I had to admit that I was still teaching part of the wrong order without noticing.\"\n\n\"That's a different kind of courage.\"\n\n\"Not an exciting one.\"\n\n\"Most useful courage isn't.\"\n\nHe smiled at that.\n\nWe watched the evening settle over Brasswatch Yard. Workers were closing the safe storage shed, and the hand pump was being checked one last time before the night rota began.\n\n\"Do you think Renn will come back tomorrow?\" he asked.\n\n\"Probably. You paid them, listened, and let them correct you.\"\n\n\"That seems like a low standard.\"\n\n\"It's a beginning.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"I want to build something people can trust.\"\n\n\"Then keep asking them what works.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"Do you trust me?\"\n\nThe question was quiet enough that I almost missed it.\n\n\"I trust you to tell me the truth about what you're trying to change,\" I said. \"And I trust myself to tell you when something still hurts. I don't need to pretend the past is harmless to love you now.\"\n\nHe looked at me for a long moment.\n\nThen he lifted my hand to his lips.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For not calling it finished.\"\n\nI leaned close. He kissed me slowly, with none of the stiffness that had once made him hold every tender gesture like a military inspection. His hand rested gently against my cheek, and I smiled into the kiss.\n\n\"Come home,\" I whispered.\n\nHe nodded.\n\nBefore the workshop closed, Ash found a line that still made them uneasy.\n\n“The complaint process goes to the shift supervisor,” they said. “What happens if the complaint is about the shift supervisor?”\n\nDanse took the page back. He had written it as though the person in authority could always be trusted to examine their own conduct. I saw the moment he recognized the old Brotherhood assumption beneath the neat wording.\n\n“Then there must be another reviewer,” he said.\n\n“Who?”\n\n“Someone selected by the workers.”\n\n“Not appointed by you?”\n\n“No.”\n\nRenn looked up from the tool drawer. “Can the trainee ask for an outside witness if the whole workshop has been involved?”\n\nDanse hesitated. “That would complicate a timely investigation.”\n\n“That is sometimes the point,” Ash said.\n\nHe crossed out the line and replaced it with a process that named the independent reviewer, the right to bring a witness and the duty to record evidence without publishing someone's personal history. It did not promise every complaint would be upheld. It promised that a complaint could be heard without going straight back to the person it concerned.\n\nWhen he showed me the revised page, the corner was stained with graphite where he'd erased his first answer.\n\n“You're making a mess of your own handbook,” I said.\n\n“So I am.”\n\n“You okay with that?”\n\nHe gave a faint, reluctant smile. “The original was defective. I would have rejected a component in the same condition.”\n\nI held his gaze for a moment. He was still the man who thought in procedures; now he was finally letting people test them.\n\n\nWe left the old handbook on the table, marked for revision.\n\nThe following morning, Renn arrived with two new questions and a better drawing of the emergency exit.\n\nDanse greeted them at the door.\n\n\"Good morning,\" he said. \"What have I missed?\"\n\nRenn grinned.\n\n\"Plenty.\"\n\nHe held the door open.\n\n\"Then let's get to work.\"","word_count":1668}},"after-always":{"after-always-12":{"number":12,"title":"What He Does Not Say","content":"Once I learned to hear Snape's silences, he became almost unbearably talkative.\n\nNot in words.\n\nNever in words if a glare would do.\n\nBut there were different kinds of quiet.\n\nThe quiet when he was concentrating had weight but no edge.\n\nThe quiet when pain climbed his throat made his shoulders lift toward his ears.\n\nThe quiet when someone mentioned Dumbledore was complicated enough to deserve its own vocabulary.\n\nAnd the quiet when children passed the dungeon door was the strangest of all.\n\nHogwarts was not yet open for term, but families had begun arriving to collect belongings left behind during the battle. Former students came with parents, siblings, guardians, and the peculiar bravery of people returning to a place where they had once been afraid.\n\nMost avoided the dungeons.\n\nSome did not.\n\nA boy of perhaps seventeen appeared in the doorway one afternoon while Snape and I were sorting preserved fluxweed.\n\nThe boy stopped so abruptly his mother nearly walked into him.\n\nSnape looked up.\n\nThe boy went pale.\n\n“Professor.”\n\nSnape's face became unreadable.\n\n“Mr. Hopkins.”\n\nI knew the name from the evidence notes. Hufflepuff. Detention under the Carrows. Broken wrist, healed badly because he had refused to report who had cursed him.\n\nHis mother looked between them.\n\n“We only came for his cauldron,” she said.\n\n“Storage room four,” Snape replied.\n\nHis voice was formal and cold.\n\nThe boy did not move.\n\nNeither did Snape.\n\nThen Hopkins said, “You changed the detention rota.”\n\nSnape's hand stopped over the jar.\n\nHis mother frowned. “What?”\n\nThe boy kept looking at him.\n\n“In March. They were making us practice curses on first-years. You changed the list and put me in inventory with Filch.”\n\nSnape's expression did not move.\n\n“You were deficient in practical organization.”\n\nHopkins swallowed.\n\n“You did it three times.”\n\n“Then your deficiency was persistent.”\n\nThe boy gave a shaky laugh.\n\nHis mother covered her mouth.\n\nSnape looked down at the fluxweed.\n\n“Storage room four,” he repeated.\n\nHopkins nodded.\n\n“Thank you,” he said.\n\nSnape's fingers tightened around the jar.\n\n“Your cauldron,” he said, “has been awaiting collection for months. I suggest you finally demonstrate responsibility.”\n\nThe boy smiled.\n\nActually smiled.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nThey left.\n\nI waited until their footsteps faded.\n\nSnape resumed sorting.\n\nI resumed with him.\n\nFive minutes passed.\n\nThen ten.\n\nI said, “You could have said you're welcome.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Too dangerous?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI glanced at him.\n\nHe was not joking.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because gratitude is rarely simple after war.”\n\n“That sounded suspiciously wise.”\n\n“It was practical.”\n\n“You don't want him to owe you.”\n\nSnape's eyes flicked toward mine.\n\nThere it was.\n\nOne of the silences.\n\nI understood.\n\n“You don't,” I said.\n\n“He owes me nothing.”\n\n“You helped him.”\n\n“I reduced his exposure to people I permitted to remain in this school.”\n\nThe words were brutal.\n\nToward himself.\n\n“You didn't permit the Carrows,” I said.\n\n“I was Headmaster.”\n\n“Under Voldemort.”\n\n“I occupied the office.”\n\n“You were also trying to keep students alive.”\n\n“And failed in ways you have not catalogued.”\n\nI set down the jar.\n\n“This is what you do.”\n\nHis expression chilled.\n\n“What, precisely?”\n\n“You turn responsibility into total responsibility. If you couldn't prevent everything, then you act as if anything that happened belongs to you.”\n\nHis voice lowered. “Do not absolve me.”\n\n“I am not.”\n\nThe force of my answer surprised both of us.\n\n“I am not absolving you,” I repeated. “You were cruel to students before the war. You protected Slytherins when they were wrong. You humiliated people who couldn't fight back. You joined Voldemort. You gave him information that led to a family being targeted. Those things are yours.”\n\nHis face had gone white.\n\nI kept going because stopping would make the honesty worse.\n\n“So are the altered detention rosters. The fake compliance draughts. The warnings. The counter-curses. The things you did when nobody could thank you. You do not get to keep only the worst evidence because it fits the sentence you've already passed on yourself.”\n\nFor a moment I thought he might hex me.\n\nInstead he laughed.\n\nIt was a terrible sound.\n\n“You imagine I have sentenced myself?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“To what?”\n\n“Survival without appeal.”\n\nThe room became utterly still.\n\nSnape's eyes fixed on me.\n\nI could not tell whether he hated me.\n\nI could tell I had reached something true.\n\nHe stood.\n\nHis chair scraped stone.\n\n“Enough.”\n\nI nodded.\n\nThis time, I let him leave.\n\nHe returned two hours later carrying a paper-wrapped parcel.\n\nHe set it on my table.\n\n“What is this?” I asked.\n\n“Food.”\n\n“I can see that.”\n\n“Then your question is redundant.”\n\nI unwrapped it.\n\nTwo meat pies. Still warm.\n\n“You went to Hogsmeade?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“For pies?”\n\n“No. I went to Hogsmeade to contemplate the unbearable persistence of your personality. The pies were incidental.”\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe sat across from me.\n\nNeither of us mentioned the argument.\n\nWe ate.\n\nHalfway through, he said, “Hopkins was not the only one.”\n\nI looked up.\n\nSnape stared at his untouched second half of pie.\n\n“There were students I could move. Students I could not. Punishments I could redirect. Others I could only shorten.”\n\nHis voice was controlled, but the words came slowly.\n\n“I kept the Carrows from using certain curses in the Great Hall by convincing them public displays made the students less afraid, not more. I assigned Hagrid duties when I could. Filch when I could. I altered records so some detentions appeared completed.”\n\nHe swallowed.\n\n“There were days when none of it mattered.”\n\nI did not interrupt.\n\n“There were children hurt in a castle I was supposedly running.”\n\n“Were you supposed to be able to stop Voldemort alone?”\n\nHis lip curled. “Do not be ridiculous.”\n\n“Then stop grading yourself on that standard.”\n\nHe looked at me sharply.\n\nI took another bite of pie.\n\n“Occupational hazard,” I said. “I recognize unreasonable quality control.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nThe room softened.\n\nAfter lunch, we returned to work.\n\nNear the end of the day, another former student passed the open door.\n\nShe was older than Hopkins, perhaps already graduated before the final year. She saw Snape and stopped.\n\nHer expression held no gratitude.\n\nOnly anger.\n\n“You,” she said.\n\nSnape's face became blank.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You made my brother cry for two years.”\n\nI went still.\n\nShe continued before anyone could stop her.\n\n“You called him incompetent in front of everyone. He stopped taking Potions because of you. He wanted to be a Healer.”\n\nSnape did not defend himself.\n\nThe woman waited.\n\nSo did I.\n\nAt last he said, “What was his name?”\n\nShe told him.\n\nRecognition crossed his face.\n\n“Yes,” he said.\n\nThe word was quiet.\n\n“You remember?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well?”\n\nSnape's hands tightened behind his back.\n\n“I was cruel to him.”\n\nThe woman looked startled.\n\nSo was I.\n\nHe continued, each word precise.\n\n“There was no educational justification for it. If he wishes to submit a formal complaint to the Board regarding my conduct, I will not contest the factual basis.”\n\nHer anger shifted, not disappearing but losing its footing.\n\n“He doesn't want a complaint.”\n\n“Then I cannot offer him what he does not want.”\n\n“You could apologize.”\n\nSnape's face changed.\n\nThere it was again.\n\nA silence that spoke.\n\nHe struggled.\n\nNot because he did not know the word.\n\nBecause saying it would make the harm real in a different way.\n\n“I am sorry,” he said.\n\nThe woman stared at him.\n\nThen nodded once.\n\n“I'll tell him.”\n\nShe walked away.\n\nSnape did not move.\n\nI looked down at my notes.\n\nI gave him privacy without leaving.\n\nSeveral minutes later, he said, “Do not.”\n\n“Do not what?”\n\n“Look pleased.”\n\nI smiled at the parchment.\n\n“I would never.”\n\n“You are intolerable.”\n\n“Accurate.”\n\nHe sat.\n\nThe next morning, I found a note slipped beneath the laboratory door. It wasn't addressed to me. The woman who'd confronted Severus had written that her brother did not want a visit or an apology sent through his sister. He wanted the school to correct a report that had described him as unwilling to study.\n\nSeverus read the note once and set it on the bench.\n\n“She is not asking you to make him feel better,” I said.\n\n“I can read.”\n\n“Then what are you going to do?”\n\n“Determine whether the report still exists.”\n\nHe sent a formal request to the records office, not to the brother. He didn't ask for the man's address. When the registrar replied that the entry had been copied into an old academic summary, Severus wrote a correction limited to what he could actually attest: the student had left the subject after repeated public humiliation, not from a lack of interest or effort. He did not claim to know the career the student might otherwise have had.\n\n“Will that make a difference?” I asked.\n\n“It may prevent someone else treating a false entry as a fact.”\n\nHe spoke with the clipped irritation he reserved for work that had been done carelessly. Then he put the pen down and looked at me.\n\n“I cannot ask him to accept the correction.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“I find that intolerable.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe signed the request anyway. No grand speech, no relieved embrace, no verdict about whether he was now good. The woman could tell her brother what had been done or leave the message unopened. Severus had made the record more accurate without recruiting a person he'd hurt to witness his improvement.\n\n\nWe finished the fluxweed in silence.\n\nBy then I knew enough to hear what he did not say.\n\nAfter the woman left, Snape's hands remained motionless on the table.\n\nI knew better than to ask who she was immediately.\n\nEventually he said, “Her son was in Slytherin.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“I accused him of attempting to attract attention when he reported harassment from older students.”\n\nMy stomach tightened.\n\n“Were they harassing him?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe word was blunt.\n\n“What happened?”\n\n“I discovered it later. I punished them.” He looked at the fluxweed as if it were safer than my face. “By then he had learned something from me that I did not intend to teach.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“That asking for help was humiliating.”\n\nThere was nothing useful to say to that.\n\nSo I did not tell him he had been under pressure. I did not remind him of the war. I did not offer context he had not asked for.\n\n“What would you do now?” I asked instead.\n\nHis eyes lifted.\n\n“Believe the report long enough to investigate it.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“Do not praise me for discovering the foundational principle of taking children seriously.”\n\n“I wasn't going to.”\n\n“You were thinking loudly.”\n\nI smiled despite myself. “Maybe a little.”\n\nHe looked almost offended, which was better than looking hollow.\n\nSometimes it sounded very much like change.","word_count":1778},"after-always-25":{"number":25,"title":"The One I Refuse to Pity","content":"The Daily Prophet printed a photograph of Snape under the headline MONSTER, MARTYR, OR BOTH?\n\nHe threw the paper into the fire.\n\nI rescued it.\n\n“Why?” he demanded.\n\n“Evidence of bad punctuation.”\n\n“There is no punctuation in the headline.”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nHe stared.\n\nI folded the paper and put it in my case.\n\nThe article was worse than the headline.\n\nIt described Snape as a tragic figure, a secret hero, a cruel teacher, a love-struck recluse, a former terrorist, a tortured genius, and—most offensively to him—“the broken man who never recovered from losing his childhood sweetheart.”\n\nSnape read that line twice.\n\nThen he laughed so coldly I felt sorry for the journalist.\n\n“Broken,” he said.\n\n“Journalists like simple nouns.”\n\n“And childhood sweetheart.”\n\n“That is inaccurate?”\n\n“It is idiotic.”\n\n“Fair.”\n\nHe stalked across the laboratory.\n\nI waited for the anger to spend itself.\n\nIt did not.\n\n“They know nothing,” he said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“They take one memory and build a religion from it.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“They speak of grief as though it were romantic constancy instead of—”\n\nHe stopped.\n\n“Instead of what?”\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n“Complicated.”\n\n“That word again.”\n\n“You overuse it.”\n\n“You embody it.”\n\nHis lip curled.\n\nI took the paper back out.\n\n“Do you want to respond?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Do you want me to burn it now?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI held it over the fire.\n\nThen paused.\n\n“What?” he snapped.\n\n“I want to ask something first.”\n\n“Of course you do.”\n\n“Do you think loving Lily ruined your life?”\n\nThe anger vanished.\n\nSnape stared at me.\n\n“That is not what the article says.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You are extrapolating.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis face closed.\n\nI nearly let it go.\n\nThen he said, “No.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“Losing her did not ruin my life,” he said. “My choices did considerable work before that.”\n\nThere was the familiar cruelty toward himself, but also something more balanced beneath it.\n\n“And loving her?”\n\nHis gaze moved toward the window.\n\n“Loving her saved parts of it.”\n\nThe answer was beautiful because it was not clean.\n\nI burned the newspaper.\n\nThe flames took monster first, then martyr.\n\nThe Prophet's headline had made me angry in ways I didn't entirely trust. It was tempting to make the argument simple: the newspaper was wrong, Severus had been hurt, therefore he deserved only kindness now.\n\nHe knew better than that. So did I.\n\n\"I don't want to write a response for you,\" I said. \"But would you like someone to request the evidence behind their claims?\"\n\nHe looked at the folded paper in my case. \"You would enjoy doing that.\"\n\n\"I might. That doesn't mean you want it done.\"\n\nHe considered for a long moment. \"Not tonight.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nI closed my case, leaving the remaining clipped notes where they were. He seemed almost startled that the conversation could stop where he asked it to. I could still hear the fire crackling behind us, the paper going soft as it burned.\n\n\"May I ask why?\" he said.\n\n\"Why I stopped?\"\n\n\"Why you didn't insist that public slander must be answered.\"\n\n\"Because you get to decide which battles are yours. I don't have to rescue you to prove I care about you.\"\n\nHis mouth tightened. \"People usually insist that I need rescuing only after they've decided I am incapable of judgment.\"\n\n\"I think you're capable of terrible judgment.\"\n\n\"How reassuring.\"\n\n\"And good judgment. Sometimes both before lunch.\"\n\nA laugh escaped him before he could stop it.\n\nI was aware of how close we were standing. My heart had started behaving as though the room were getting warmer. I didn't want to turn anger at a newspaper into an excuse to close the space between us.\n\n\"I like you,\" I said. \"Not because I think I can fix what happened. Not because I want the world to forgive you through me.\"\n\n\"I had rather suspected you of poor taste.\"\n\n\"You'd be right.\"\n\nHe looked down at my hands. I wanted him to reach for them. I also wanted the choice to mean something when he did.\n\nWe left the paper unanswered that evening. In the morning, he could decide whether to challenge a claim or let it go. For now he was just a difficult man standing in front of me, and I was allowed to want him without making that want an argument for his innocence.\n\nSnape watched.\n\n“I refuse to pity you,” I said.\n\nHis eyebrow lifted.\n\n“What an extraordinary gift.”\n\n“I mean it.”\n\n“I know. That is what makes it extraordinary.”\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n“I feel compassion for you sometimes.”\n\n“How unfortunate.”\n\n“I feel angry on your behalf sometimes.”\n\n“Worse.”\n\n“I think some things that happened to you were terrible.”\n\n“You are approaching sentimentality.”\n\n“And I still refuse to pity you.”\n\nHis expression sharpened.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because pity puts you below me.”\n\nThat stopped him.\n\n“I don't want you below me,” I continued. “I want you beside me.”\n\nSilence.\n\nThe words came out more intimate than I expected.\n\nSnape's eyes locked on mine.\n\nI could hear the fire behind us.\n\nI could hear my own pulse.\n\nBeside me.\n\nNot save you.\n\nNot fix you.\n\nNot forgive you for things that were not mine to forgive.\n\nBeside me.\n\nSnape took one step forward.\n\nNow we were too close for pretense.\n\n“Do you?” he asked.\n\nHis voice was barely above a whisper.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You have poor judgment.”\n\n“Established.”\n\n“You have seen enough to know that.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You are still here.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis hand lifted.\n\nStopped beside my face.\n\n“May I?”\n\nEvery thought in my head disappeared.\n\nI nodded.\n\n“Say it.”\n\nThe demand was soft.\n\nConsent mattered to him in language.\n\nMaybe because so much of his life had been built around coercion, orders, secrets, things no one could safely refuse.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis fingers touched my cheek.\n\nNothing more.\n\nNo kiss.\n\nJust the back of his knuckles against my skin, careful enough to break me open.\n\nI leaned into the touch.\n\nSnape inhaled sharply.\n\nHis thumb moved once below my cheekbone.\n\n“You are real,” he said.\n\nI blinked.\n\n“So are you.”\n\nHis mouth twisted.\n\n“Debatable.”\n\nI laughed softly.\n\nThe tension eased without disappearing.\n\nHis hand stayed.\n\n“I thought,” he said, “that once you saw enough, this would correct itself.”\n\n“This?”\n\n“Your attraction.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“That is incredibly insulting.”\n\n“It was intended as self-criticism.”\n\n“You're bad at keeping those separate.”\n\n“I am learning.”\n\nThe honesty of it made my chest ache.\n\nI raised my own hand, giving him enough time to refuse.\n\nHe did not.\n\nI touched the side of his face.\n\nHis eyes closed.\n\nThat nearly undid me.\n\nSnape, who watched everything.\n\nSnape, who slept with a wand close enough to wake armed.\n\nSnape closed his eyes under my hand.\n\nTrust could be very quiet.\n\nI stroked my thumb once along his cheek.\n\nHis skin was cool. A little rough. Human.\n\nWhen he opened his eyes again, there was desire in them.\n\nNo mistaking it.\n\nMy breath caught.\n\nHis gaze dropped to my mouth.\n\nThen returned to my eyes.\n\nHe stepped back.\n\nI let him.\n\nThe absence of contact hurt immediately.\n\n“Not yet,” he said.\n\nI nodded.\n\n“Okay.”\n\nSurprise crossed his face.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You agree rather readily.”\n\n“You said not yet, not convince me.”\n\nHis expression softened.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThere was a little glass vial resting at the edge of the bench. Its label had begun to peel where steam from the cauldron reached it. I picked it up, turning it so the light struck the ink.\n\n“Wrong shelf,” Severus said.\n\n“You're supposed to be having an emotional conversation.”\n\n“I can have one without confusing powdered asphodel with crushed moonstone.”\n\n“Can you?”\n\n“Apparently not with you rearranging the ingredients.”\n\nI put the vial back exactly where he'd told me and watched him take a clean label from the drawer. His left hand was less steady than he'd wanted me to notice. When the paper stuck to his thumb, he made a sound of such controlled disgust that I almost laughed.\n\n“Would you like help?”\n\nHe looked at the label and then at me. “I would like a reliable pair of hands.”\n\n“Mine are available. On a voluntary basis.”\n\nHe let me hold the glass while he aligned the new paper. We worked close enough that my sleeve brushed his, and I could smell the sharp, clean bitterness of the herbs. I was aware of his breathing. He was aware of mine. Neither of us moved away, but when he finally stepped back he gave me room to decide whether to follow.\n\n“Thank you,” he said.\n\n“For what?”\n\n“For not asking me to perform gratitude.”\n\nI set the vial in its marked place. The article had reduced his whole life to two possible headlines. We had spent five minutes repairing a label, and somehow that demanded more attention to who he actually was.\n\n\nI turned toward the worktable, giving both of us something else to look at.\n\n“Also, the aconite needs sorting.”\n\nSnape made a sound of disbelief.\n\n“You are changing the subject to aconite.”\n\n“You started this relationship with bitterroot.”\n\n“We do not have a relationship.”\n\nI looked over my shoulder.\n\nHis face changed.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“No?”\n\nSnape stared at me.\n\nThen, very slowly, his mouth curved.\n\n“Sort the aconite.”\n\n“Coward.”\n\n“Consultant.”\n\n“Prince.”\n\nHis eyes warmed.\n\nWe sorted aconite.\n\nThe newspaper turned to ash behind us.\n\nMonster and martyr disappeared together.\n\nThe almost-kiss followed me for the rest of the afternoon.\n\nIt lived in absurd places: between ingredient counts, in the pause before a cabinet opened, in the half second when Severus passed behind my chair and his sleeve brushed my shoulder.\n\nNeither of us mentioned it.\n\nEventually I put down my quill.\n\n“This is ridiculous.”\n\n“What is?”\n\n“You know.”\n\n“I am not responsible for every undefined discomfort you experience.”\n\n“You touched my face.”\n\nHis quill stopped.\n\n“Ah,” he said.\n\n“Ah?”\n\n“A highly technical response.”\n\nI stared at him until he sighed.\n\n“I did not stop because I did not want to kiss you.”\n\nMy pulse jumped.\n\n“Then why?”\n\n“Because I wanted to be certain the moment was not powered by pity, crisis, gratitude, or your appalling desire to rehabilitate difficult men.”\n\n“I do not have an appalling desire to rehabilitate difficult men.”\n\nHis eyebrow rose.\n\n“Fine. One difficult man.”\n\n“That is not reassuring.”\n\nI smiled despite the heat in my face.\n\n“For the record,” I said, “I wanted to kiss you because you're infuriating.”\n\n“Excellent. Romance restored to its proper foundation.”\n\nThe tension broke just enough for us to breathe.\n\nNot yet, I understood, was not the same as no.\n\nThe man remained.","word_count":1753},"after-always-47":{"number":47,"title":"A Teacher, Not a Verdict","content":"The apprentice arrived carrying a notebook filled with Severus Snape's heroic deeds.\n\nThat was not what they'd intended, of course. They believed they had compiled a respectful account of a complicated man. The fact that it sounded like an advertisement for sainthood had escaped them.\n\n\"My name is Orin,\" they said, offering a hand. \"I'm studying historical testimony for the Moonroot oral-history program.\"\n\nSeverus looked at the notebook.\n\n\"How unfortunate.\"\n\nOrin smiled nervously.\n\n\"I've read about your work during the war.\"\n\n\"Have you?\"\n\n\"Yes. I think you were one of the greatest heroes of your generation.\"\n\nThe silence was extraordinary.\n\nI was arranging copies of the Closed Bells report on the shelf. I stopped moving. From the corner of my eye, I saw Severus's expression settle into something very still.\n\n\"On what evidence?\" he asked.\n\nOrin opened the notebook.\n\n\"Well, you were a double agent. You protected students. You made sacrifices no one understood until afterward.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And you were misunderstood.\"\n\n\"Was I?\"\n\nOrin looked at me for help.\n\nI set down the papers.\n\n\"Why don't you tell him which sources you used?\"\n\nThey listed several popular histories, an article written shortly after the war, and a memoir by somebody who had met Severus twice. None of the material was wholly invented. Some of it was incomplete. All of it treated the ending as though it retroactively explained every choice that came before.\n\nSeverus took the notebook, with permission, and turned to the first page.\n\n\"Here you say I had no choice.\"\n\nOrin nodded.\n\n\"That was the tragic part.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"That is the part you have mistaken.\"\n\nThe apprentice looked wounded.\n\nSeverus returned the notebook.\n\n\"I made choices. Some were constrained by threats. Some were made in ignorance. Others were wrong. If you remove my agency, you do not make me admirable. You make the harm inexplicable.\"\n\nOrin swallowed.\n\n\"I didn't mean to insult you.\"\n\n\"You have not insulted me. You have made a bad argument.\"\n\nI bit back a smile.\n\nIt wasn't a gentle conversation. But Severus was teaching, not sentencing. I could see him making an effort to leave the apprentice room to correct themself.\n\n\"Would you show me where it goes wrong?\" Orin asked.\n\nSeverus looked surprised.\n\nThen he pulled out a chair.\n\n\"Sit.\"\n\nFor the next hour, he took the notebook apart line by line.\n\nHe asked which claims had witnesses. Which were based on later interpretation. Which events had harmed people, regardless of his reasons. Where Orin had substituted an adjective for an explanation.\n\n\"Brave is not a substitute for precise,\" he said.\n\nOrin wrote that down.\n\nThey worked without an audience until Neri came through the doorway to retrieve the ledger report. Neri paused, listened, and quietly continued to the other room.\n\nI brought tea.\n\nSeverus accepted his cup without breaking the sentence he was explaining.\n\nOrin drank theirs too quickly and burned their tongue.\n\n\"Careful,\" I said.\n\nThey winced.\n\nSeverus looked at the cup.\n\n\"An instructive example of consequences arriving before understanding.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nOrin laughed too, after a moment.\n\nBy afternoon, the notebook had more corrections than original lines. Orin looked exhausted.\n\n\"Do you still think I should write the piece?\" they asked.\n\n\"That depends on whether you intend to revise it.\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"Then write it.\"\n\n\"What should I call you?\"\n\nSeverus considered.\n\n\"Someone who made choices and must answer for them.\"\n\n\"That's a terrible title.\"\n\n\"An honest one.\"\n\nI watched Orin think.\n\n\"Maybe that's the point,\" they said.\n\nSeverus gave the smallest nod.\n\nBefore Orin went, Neri returned with a sealed paper sleeve.\n\n\"One of your references contains testimony given confidentially,\" they said. \"You may document that a statement was received. You may not publish its contents.\"\n\nOrin frowned. \"But I need the detail to support my argument.\"\n\n\"Then you require a different argument,\" Severus said.\n\n\"How can I tell the truth if I leave things out?\"\n\n\"By declining to steal someone else's truth.\"\n\nThe words landed harder than either of us expected. Severus stared at the papers in his hands. He had spent a lifetime knowing what secrets could cost, and he was trying not to turn that knowledge into another weapon.\n\nOrin considered the sleeve. \"Could I ask the witness?\"\n\n\"If they can be approached without identifying them,\" Neri said. \"And if you are ready to hear no.\"\n\nOrin pulled the confidential quotation from the draft. We found two publicly available accounts that supported part of the chronology but not the detail they wanted to claim. Orin wrote UNKNOWN beside the unresolved date.\n\n\"People will think I didn't do my research,\" they said.\n\n\"Better that than pretending the evidence is complete,\" Severus answered.\n\nI watched him turn the notebook toward the apprentice rather than reaching across to correct it himself.\n\n\"Your work must be yours,\" he said. \"So must your mistakes.\"\n\nOrin made the change in their own handwriting. Their paper was less flattering, much more difficult, and finally honest.\n\n\nWhen the apprentice left, they took their notebook and a stack of primary-source references. No blessing. No grand declaration. Just work to do.\n\nI was proud of Severus, but I waited until the archive was nearly empty before telling him.\n\n\"You didn't let them turn you into a monument,\" I said.\n\n\"I have never cared for statues.\"\n\n\"Toph would approve.\"\n\n\"That is a deeply unsettling endorsement.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nSeverus gathered the loose papers into a careful stack.\n\n\"Orin will probably write something foolish anyway,\" he said.\n\n\"Maybe. But they asked to learn. You gave them a way.\"\n\nHe looked down at his hands.\n\n\"I used to think teaching was the art of making students fear mistakes.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I suspect it is the art of making them capable of recognizing one.\"\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"That sounds suspiciously kind.\"\n\n\"Do not spread the rumor.\"\n\nThe evening light caught the edge of his hair. There were dark smudges of ink on his fingers, and he had forgotten to remove his outer robe after the morning's catalog work. I brushed a speck of dust from his shoulder.\n\nHe watched me.\n\n\"You're staring.\"\n\n\"Am I?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I like you.\"\n\n\"An alarming lapse of judgment.\"\n\n\"I've been making it for some time.\"\n\nHe tried to look severe. His mouth betrayed him.\n\nI took his hand and led him toward the little reading nook behind the archive's west room. It was empty, quiet, and warm enough to escape the draft from the main hall.\n\nSeverus stopped beside the shelf.\n\n\"Jasper.\"\n\n\"Yes?\"\n\n\"I had expected you to find the discussion tiresome.\"\n\n\"I found it difficult. That's not the same.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"I know you have many people you care about, and I know we have agreed what that means for us. Sometimes I still wonder whether you'd rather spend your evenings with someone less complicated.\"\n\nI felt a small ache in my chest.\n\n\"Severus, you're not a problem I agreed to solve. You're someone I choose. That doesn't require you to become easy.\"\n\nHe let out a slow breath.\n\n\"And if I remain profoundly unpleasant?\"\n\n\"Then I'll tell you when you're being unpleasant.\"\n\n\"How romantic.\"\n\n\"Would you like a demonstration?\"\n\nHis eyebrows rose.\n\nI kissed him before he could find a better insult. He answered with a warmth that made me forget the smell of old paper for a moment. His fingers settled against my waist, asking without words whether I wanted him closer.\n\nI did.\n\nOrin brought the second draft to the desk before the archive closed. At the back was a list of names gathered from Severus's old school records.\n\n“I thought I should interview them,” Orin said. “If the history needs more than one voice.”\n\nSeverus read the list without touching it. “Have any of these people asked to participate?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then you have compiled a set of people to be bothered about the worst years of their lives. A remarkable feat of scholarship.”\n\nOrin flushed. I touched the corner of the paper, not to rescue them, but to keep the discussion from becoming another public humiliation.\n\n“Some might want to speak,” I said. “They should get an invitation that lets them say no without explaining why.”\n\nNeri came over with the archive's standard contact form. It gave potential witnesses control over whether to speak, how to be quoted and what records they wanted kept closed. A blank reply was not consent. There would be no repeated letters after a refusal.\n\nOrin read it twice. “I was trying to make the record fair.”\n\n“Then begin,” Severus said, “by not assigning your research subjects a duty to make it fair for you.”\n\nThey struck several names from the preliminary interview list, including the people whose current addresses had been obtained from private correspondence. Severus checked the final form for ambiguous wording, then put his pen down.\n\n“I cannot certify the quality of what people will tell you,” he said. “I can only tell you where my own account is incomplete.”\n\nOrin nodded, older in that moment than when they'd arrived with a notebook full of praise. The archive would not finish the story on schedule. It would give the people named in it a choice.\n\n\nWe stayed until the last lamp in the archive was turned down, then went home together. The evening was private and unhurried, the kind of intimacy that needed no witness or verdict.\n\nThe next morning, a note from Orin arrived at Moonroot.\n\nThey had rewritten the opening.\n\nIt began, not with an accusation or a hymn of praise, but with a question about the difference between what a person intended and what their choices did.\n\nSeverus read it and set it on the table.\n\n\"Better,\" he said.\n\nI smiled.\n\nFor him, that was practically applause.","word_count":1622}}};for(const s of lib.series){const entries=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=entries[ch.id];if(e&&e.number===ch.chapter_number){ch.title=e.title;ch.content=e.content;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}}})();
